projects.description:
Within the framework of the project "Introduction of Energy Management System Standards in Ukrainian Industry"

In order to achieve the goal of increased energy efficiency in industrial sectors, the project interventions will stimulate the demand for energy-efficient services through:
• The formulation and implementation of enabling policy and regulatory frameworks for EnMS and ESO adoption;
• The creation of the necessary institutional capacity to implement;
programmes on EnMS, awareness-raising, energy audits and demonstration projects;
• Support the supply of energy-efficient services by building the institutional capacities to accredit and certify EnMS compliance under the ISO50001 standard;
• Training local trainers and consultants in EnMS implementation and ESO;
• Putting in place a financial incentive mechanism to support the uptake of EnMS and ESO within the project pilot enterprise programme.

Deliverables in Ukraine will include:
List and detailed specification of laboratory equipment and consumables to be procured;
Manual and study materials for simulation exercises on African Swine Fever and at least one other animal disease submitted to relevant authority;
6 simulation exercises conducted by relevant authority in cooperation with FAO;
At least 3 trainings for hunters conducted at a pilot site;
Note on Ukrainian veterinary education comparison with OIE Recommendations with  recommendations;
Work plan on improvement of veterinary curriculum developed and discussed with three universities with the support in implementation of the work plan;
2 research vouchers of up to USD 5,000 provided;
Biosafety audit conducted, recommendations developed and direct technical support provided to at least 3 private companies;
2 national and 1 international conferences conducted;
3 workshops and/or round tables conducted;
1 study tours organized, free of charge participation for 5 representatives of public sector or agribusiness associations granted;
50 awareness raising events for local communities, households, small producers, local veterinarians, hunting community conducted;
Educational and informational package disseminated to 30 schools and 5 universities;
Meat market analysis note prepared and http://meatbalance.org.ua/ site supported; 
Voluntary quality assurance scheme developed and discussed with at least one industry association;
2 press conferences conducted, 4 press-releases released, 4 articles published.

Forestry and the wood processing industry (primary and secondary processing of timber) accounts for 1 percent of the GDP of Ukraine. As a whole, the economic, social and environmental functions of forests are of huge importance for sustainable development of the society and improvement of the quality of life, particularly in the rural areas and environmental services.
Although Ukraine has a great potential for forestry development, there are new emerging limiting factors related to global environmental processes, resulting in a highly increased proportion of dead forest stands and damage to plantings by pests and diseases. The preliminary findings suggested that reduced vitality of pine forests as the primary cause for the susceptibility of trees to bark beetle attack and the subsequent dieback. 

FAO will focus on addressing the below described issue through the following actions:
•	national surveillance and monitoring systems revised to develop functional early warning systems; 
•	national contingency plans for forest pest outbreaks developed and validated with regional and district forest managers;  
•	guidelines developed for improving resilience of forest stands in relation to forest health and enhancing drought mitigation;
•	national capacities on communication and regional coordination in relations to forest health issues strengthened;
•	national capacities strengthened to increase the resilience of forest ecosystems to unfavorable biotic and abiotic factors.

Following ITU's council resolution 1408 on "Assistance and support to Ukraine for rebuilding their telecommunication sector", a series of activities are being carried out in order to: 
1. to monitor and provide regular reports on the particular needs of Ukraine in the field of telecommunications, and to prepare proposals for effective technical assistance;
2. to carry out an assessment on the impact of the war in Ukraine on ITU programmes and activities in the region and provide a report thereon;
3. to ensure adequate financial and human resources mobilization, including under the internal budget and the Information and Communication Technology Development Fund, for the implementation of the proposed actions;
4. to coordinate the activities carried out by the three Sectors of the Union in accordance with resolves above, to ensure that the Union's action in favour of Ukraine is as effective as possible, and to provide a report on the matter to the 2022 Plenipotentiary conference and to the Council meeting in 2023, and future meetings and conferences as appropriate;
5. to make contributions to the WTDC-21 and PP-22, RDF-23 to support ITU efforts to rebuild Ukraine’s telecommunication infrastructure and necessary support and technical capacity building.

Activities as of 2023 have been focused on supporting the telecommunication infrastructure reconstruction.
